WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:14.382
you are the most exasperating girl!

2
00:00:15.263 --> 00:00:16.824
What is it that you've done?"

3
00:00:17.304 --> 00:00:18.285
Marilla exclaimed.

4
00:00:18.305 --> 00:00:20.887
"I've sold Mr.

5
00:00:20.927 --> 00:00:22.449
Harrison's cow to Mr.

6
00:00:22.549 --> 00:00:28.373
Shearer! Dolly is out in the milking pen this very minute," Anne said.

7
00:00:28.393 --> 00:00:31.196
"Anne Shirley, are you dreaming?"

8
00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:32.240
asked Marilla.

9
00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:35.521
"I wish I were," said Anne.

10
00:00:35.541 --> 00:00:38.442
"But I feel like I'm having a nightmare.

11
00:00:39.242 --> 00:00:42.923
Mr. Harrison's cow is on the other side of the island by now.

12
00:00:43.643 --> 00:00:44.504
What can I do?"

13
00:00:44.524 --> 00:00:48.785
"Anne, you must tell Mr.

14
00:00:48.825 --> 00:00:51.846
Harrison right now," Marilla said sternly.

15
00:00:51.866 --> 00:00:56.267
"I'm sure he'll be awfully angry," said Anne.

16
00:00:56.287 --> 00:00:59.808
"He probably will," said Marilla.

17
00:01:00.548 --> 00:01:02.289
"I suppose I can talk to him."

18
00:01:04.129 --> 00:01:05.750
"No," exclaimed Anne.

19
00:01:05.770 --> 00:01:08.330
"It's my fault—I'll go."

20
00:01:09.811 --> 00:01:12.612
As Anne left she saw a cake she had baked that morning.

21
00:01:13.412 --> 00:01:16.033
She decided to take it with her as a peace offering.

22
00:01:17.553 --> 00:01:21.694
Mr. Harrison was sitting on his veranda, enjoying his evening pipe.

23
00:01:22.535 --> 00:01:27.096
When he saw Anne, he sprang to his feet, ran into the house, and shut the door.

24
00:01:27.944 --> 00:01:33.267
"If he was angry before, he'll be full of rage when he hears what I've done," Anne thought

25
00:01:33.347 --> 00:01:36.889
miserably. Anne knocked on the door, and Mr.

26
00:01:36.909 --> 00:01:37.850
Harrison opened it.

27
00:01:39.371 --> 00:01:44.454
Surprisingly, he spoke in a friendly, though nervous, tone and invited Anne inside.

28
00:01:45.774 --> 00:01:48.756
Mr. Harrison politely offered Anne a dusty chair.

29
00:01:49.837 --> 00:01:56.788
Everything was quite pleasant until Ginger the parrot exclaimed, "Who's that redheaded smarty-pants?"

30
00:01:56.828 --> 00:01:59.510
It was hard to say whose face went redder: Mr.

31
00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:00.770
Harrison's or Anne's.

32
00:02:00.790 --> 00:02:04.813
"Don't mind that parrot," said Mr.

33
00:02:04.833 --> 00:02:07.254
Harrison, casting a furious glance at Ginger.

34
00:02:07.274 --> 00:02:11.076
"He's he's always talking nonsense.

35
00:02:11.696 --> 00:02:13.597
I got him from my brother who was a sailor."

36
00:02:14.858 --> 00:02:16.839
"That's all right," murmured Anne.

37
00:02:16.859 --> 00:02:20.701
"Mr. Harrison, I have something to confess.

38
00:02:21.582 --> 00:02:22.702
It's about the cow."

39
00:02:23.903 --> 00:02:25.844
"Was that cow in my oats again?

40
00:02:26.388 --> 00:02:28.349
Well, never mind," Mr.

41
00:02:28.369 --> 00:02:29.130
Harrison said.

42
00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:32.131
"I was too angry yesterday.

43
00:02:33.252 --> 00:02:35.093
Sometimes I have a bad temper.

44
00:02:35.693 --> 00:02:37.134
The oats will be all right, I'm sure."

45
00:02:37.154 --> 00:02:41.936
"Actually, it's ten times worse," Anne said.

46
00:02:41.956 --> 00:02:44.558
"Was the cow in my wheat?"

47
00:02:44.958 --> 00:02:45.538
asked Mr.

48
00:02:45.578 --> 00:02:49.681
Harrison. "No, not the wheat," Anne replied.

49
00:02:49.701 --> 00:02:52.082
"The cabbages?"

50
00:02:52.542 --> 00:02:53.102
asked Mr.

51
00:02:53.122 --> 00:02:55.744
Harrison. "My prize cabbages?"

52
00:02:56.908 --> 00:02:57.569
"No, Mr.

53
00:02:57.629 --> 00:02:59.890
Harrison," said Anne anxiously.

54
00:02:59.910 --> 00:03:04.674
"I I've sold your cow.

55
00:03:05.714 --> 00:03:08.756
It was an accident and I'm very, very sorry."

56
00:03:09.597 --> 00:03:11.298
Anne then explained what had happened.

57
00:03:11.318 --> 00:03:13.920
"Well," said Mr.

58
00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:18.523
Harrison slowly, "accidents happen, and I will take Dolly in exchange."

59
00:03:19.944 --> 00:03:22.686
Anne breathed a sigh of relief and smiled meekly.

60
00:03:22.706 --> 00:03:25.388
"Would you like some cake?"

61
00:03:27.616 --> 00:03:30.638
And so began the unlikely friendship of Mr.

62
00:03:30.678 --> 00:03:31.898
Harrison and Anne.

63
00:03:33.299 --> 00:03:37.921
A few days later, Anne, Gilbert Blythe, and Jane Andrews were walking together.

64
00:03:39.222 --> 00:03:42.503
The next day they would all start their new jobs as teachers.

65
00:03:43.624 --> 00:03:46.465
They were discussing how bad students should be punished.

66
00:03:46.485 --> 00:03:51.788
"I could never hit a child," Anne said passionately.

67
00:03:52.844 --> 00:03:57.608
"Miss Stacy never hit anyone, and she had perfect order when she taught us.

68
00:03:58.770 --> 00:04:02.573
Mr. Phillips hit students all the time, and he never had order!"

69
00:04:02.593 --> 00:04:06.296
"But what if the children don't obey?

70
00:04:06.316 --> 00:04:09.679
I think it's a good punishment," said Jane.

71
00:04:09.699 --> 00:04:12.122
"What do you think, Gilbert?"

72
00:04:12.142 --> 00:04:15.004
"Well," Gilbert said slowly.

73
00:04:15.945 --> 00:04:18.808
He agreed with Jane, but he wanted Anne's approval.

74
00:04:20.688 --> 00:04:24.550
"I don't believe in hitting children much—maybe just as a last resort."

75
00:04:25.611 --> 00:04:27.532
Anne and Jane both scowled at him.

76
00:04:28.533 --> 00:04:33.275
Gilbert had tried to please both and, as a result, he had pleased nobody.

77
00:04:34.796 --> 00:04:40.360
When Anne entered the classroom of the Avonlea School the next morning, she was confronted by rows of

78
00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:42.921
shining faces with bright, inquisitive eyes.

79
00:04:44.462 --> 00:04:47.644
Anne hoped she did not look as frightened and foolish as she felt.

80
00:04:49.244 --> 00:04:52.945
She had stayed up almost until midnight composing a speech for her students.

81
00:04:54.526 --> 00:04:55.926
She opened her mouth to begin.

82
00:04:57.486 --> 00:05:03.348
Even though it was a good speech with some very fine ideas, especially about cooperation and the joy

83
00:05:03.388 --> 00:05:06.389
of learning, Anne could not remember a word of it.

84
00:05:08.289 --> 00:05:15.631
After what seemed to her a year—about ten seconds in reality—Anne said faintly, "Take out your English books,

85
00:05:15.731 --> 00:05:17.432
please," and sat down.

86
00:05:20.064 --> 00:05:23.025
Anne looked at her new students as they read the first chapter.

87
00:05:24.706 --> 00:05:30.068
She knew most of them because, only a year ago, she had been a student herself and had

88
00:05:30.108 --> 00:05:31.128
gone to school with them.

89
00:05:32.068 --> 00:05:33.929
There were also ten new students.

90
00:05:35.069 --> 00:05:37.450
Sitting at a corner desk was Anthony Pye.

91
00:05:38.270 --> 00:05:43.052
He had a dark, sullen little face and was staring at Anne with a hostile expression.

92
00:05:44.716 --> 00:05:49.457
Three pale little girls, all crowded into one seat, were from the Cotton family.

93
00:05:51.198 --> 00:05:53.579
A handsome little boy sat in the front of the room.

94
00:05:54.459 --> 00:05:58.820
Anne thought he must be Paul Irving, whose father had once lived in Avonlea.

95
00:05:59.641 --> 00:06:03.842
When Paul's mother died, Paul had come to Avonlea to live with his grandmother.

96
00:06:05.042 --> 00:06:10.484
Anne and Paul smiled at each other, and Anne felt that each had recognized a kindred spirit in

97
00:06:10.504 --> 00:06:10.844
the other.

98
00:06:12.568 --> 00:06:17.431
Throughout the day the children behaved quite well; only two cases of discipline occurred.

99
00:06:18.531 --> 00:06:20.953
Morley Andrews was caught with a pair of crickets.

100
00:06:21.733 --> 00:06:26.736
Anne confiscated the crickets and made Morley stand on the lecture platform for an hour.

101
00:06:27.756 --> 00:06:30.678
She put the crickets in a box and later set them free.

102
00:06:31.798 --> 00:06:37.962
However, from that day on, Morley always believed Anne had taken them home and kept them as pets.

103
00:06:39.883 --> 00:06:45.200
The other culprit was Anthony Pye, who poured water down the back of the youngest Cotton girl's neck.

104
00:06:46.641 --> 00:06:53.728
Anne gave Anthony a lecture that was quite kind and touching, but Anthony remained unfazed and whistled scornfully

105
00:06:53.788 --> 00:06:54.529
when she finished.

106
00:06:55.911 --> 00:06:59.334
At three o'clock Anne rang the bell and watched her students go home.

107
00:07:00.916 --> 00:07:04.159
She resolved to love all of them, even Anthony
